  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b5d17f44-04a6-4bbd-8581-f202b846e174",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The clustering helped us divide the customers into five groups. The groups can be divided into:\n",
    "\n",
    "- number 0 high both remuneration and spending\n",
    "- number 1 low both remuneration and spending\n",
    "- number 2 high remuneration, low spending\n",
    "- number 3 low remuneartion, high spending\n",
    "- number 4 medium both remuneration and spending\n",
    "\n",
    "Number 4 (medium both remuneration and budget) is the largest and it a good base because it has medium remuneration ans spending score. Number 0 and 2 are almost half of the size of the largest, which is a good point because they both have high remuneration. Number 2 has great potential indeed beacuse of its low spending score  and could be targeted for promotion. Number 1 and 3 have both low remuneration but the first has low spending score and the latter has high spending score. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "17c6df73-f91e-441b-8b55-f1f81365382f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Summary:\n",
    "the clustering helped us identify five groups that could be targeted with different marketing promotions and we could value their different risk managment. Investigating which cluster is more likely to churn od which cluster is more profitable would be an insightful next step.\n",
    "\n",
    "Anyway, as I wrote before, the final decision about the number of clusters has to be done in accordance with the purpose of the stakeholder. Maybe they prefer to have fewer cluster and for semplicity purpose, or maybe more separated clusters to isolate outliers."
   ]
  },
